8	 * Computes the articulation points (cut vertices) and bridges (cut edges) of an
9	 * undirected graph using Tarjan's depth-first search algorithm. Both are derived
10	 * from the same low-link traversal in a single {@code O(V + E)} pass.
11	 *
12	 * <p>A vertex is an articulation point if removing it increases the number of
13	 * connected components; an edge is a bridge if removing it does. Multigraphs are
14	 * handled correctly: the traversal skips only the specific tree edge it arrived
15	 * on (by {@link Graph.Edge#id() edge id}), so a parallel edge back to the parent
16	 * is treated as a genuine back edge. This matters for bridges, whose strict
17	 * {@code low[child] > disc[parent]} test a parallel edge can flip.
18	 *
19	 * <p>Instances are stateless and therefore reusable and thread-safe: each call to
20	 * {@link #analyze(Graph)} allocates its own traversal state.
21	 *
22	 * <p>The traversal is implemented with an explicit stack rather than recursion, so
23	 * it handles arbitrarily deep graphs without risking a {@link StackOverflowError}.
24	 */
